<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Revolutionary Guards, IRGC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps is a branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s armed forces founded in 1979 to protect the Islamic Republic and its revolution. Separate from the regular army, it grew powerful during the Iran-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> War and controls the Basij militia, ballistic missiles and the Quds Force, which supports allied armed groups abroad. It also has extensive business interests.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islamic-revolutionary-guard-corps.jpg" alt="Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps" width="320" height="341" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrInfo2012 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_Army_of_the_Guardians_of_the_Islamic_Revolution.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Corps is also known as Sepah or Pasdaran, from Persian words meaning corps and guardians. It was established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhollah-khomeini">Ruhollah Khomeini</a> as a military branch in May 1979, in the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-revolution">Iranian Revolution</a>, and was formed on 5 May 1979 by Abbas Duzduzani. Its purpose was to consolidate several paramilitary forces into a single force loyal to the new government and to serve as a counter to the regular military, which was at first seen as a possible source of opposition because of its traditional loyalty to the Shah. The Constitution of the Islamic Republic entrusted the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s territorial integrity and political independence to the regular army, the Artesh, and gave the Pasdaran the task of preserving the Revolution itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Corps emerged from the Iran-Iraq War of 1980 to 1988 as a largely independent organisation on which the clerical leadership relied. Its share of the Iranian military budget was 7.3 per cent in 1980, rose to 20.3 per cent by 1982 and reached almost 50 per cent by 1987. During the war it also built a large apparatus for cultural and media production. Early 1980s reports credit it with establishing over 2,400 libraries, producing some 440,000 publications and holding 4,049 film screenings and 1,620 exhibitions.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanese-civil-war">Lebanese Civil War</a> it allegedly sent troops to train fighters after the Israeli invasion of 1982, and Christian militias such as the Lebanese Forces declared war on it, claiming it violated Lebanese sovereignty. In September 2008 it reorganised from a centralised to a decentralised force, with 31 provincial corps and an autonomous missile command. A command for cyber security was set up within it in 2007.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Corps is a multi-service force with ground, aerospace and naval branches, the Quds Force and the Basij, a volunteer militia that serves as its auxiliary. Its commander-in-chief is appointed by the supreme leader. Its operations are geared to asymmetric warfare and less traditional duties such as control of smuggling and of the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, and it is responsible for internal and border security and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s missile forces. Its aerospace branch controls the strategic-missile force, and its Quds Force trains, arms and advises allied non-state groups abroad.</p><p>Its members have taken part in politics: Mahmoud Ahmadinejad, president from 2005 to 2013, joined it in 1985, and a majority of his first cabinet were veterans. It first moved into commerce through networks of veterans and former officials, and grew into a conglomerate with interests across the Iranian economy, including contracts in oil, gas and petrochemicals and in major infrastructure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Power is shared between a Supreme Leader, an elected president and parliament, and unelected bodies such as the Guardian Council and the <b>Revolutionary Guards</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-republic-of-iran">Islamic Republic of Iran</a></li></ul></section>
